.\"
.\" GETDENTS(2) - Substrate Native Personality
.\"
.TH GETDENTS 2 "January 2026" "Substrate OS" "System Calls"
.SH NAME
getdents \- get directory entries
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <dirent.h>
.PP
.BI "int getdents(unsigned int " fd ", struct dirent *" dirp ","
.BI "             unsigned int " count ");
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR getdents ()
reads several
.I dirent
structures from the directory referred to by the open file descriptor
.I fd
into the buffer pointed to by
.IR dirp .
The argument
.I count
specifies the size of that buffer.
.SS The dirent structure
.nf
struct dirent {
    unsigned long  d_ino;     /* Inode number */
    unsigned long  d_off;     /* Offset to next entry */
    unsigned short d_reclen;  /* Length of this dirent */
    char           d_name[];  /* Filename (null-terminated) */
};
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success, the number of bytes read is returned. On end of directory,
0 is returned.
.PP
On error, \-1 is returned, and
.I errno
is set appropriately.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EBADF
.I fd
is not a valid file descriptor opened for reading.
.TP
.B ENOTDIR
.I fd
does not refer to a directory.
.SH NOTES
.SS Substrate Native ABI
.nf
Syscall Number: 141
Arguments:      fd, dirp, count (on stack)
.fi
.SS Application Interface
Applications should use
.BR opendir (3),
.BR readdir (3),
and
.BR closedir (3)
rather than calling
.BR getdents ()
directly.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR open (2),
.BR opendir (3),
.BR readdir (3),
.BR closedir (3),
.BR substrate (7)
.SH STANDARDS
Linux-specific, widely adopted.
